WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:08.360
Since he was a kid, Michael Gamez wanted to work on cars just like his dad and his grandfather.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.220
He fixed up and sold his first used car when he was fourteen.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:17.460
It felt really good to build something up and sell it for a profit. The fact that I was able to do something like that,

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.120
which was kind of like, ok you know, I know what I'm doing, this is what I want to do.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.760
But Gamez's teachers conditioned him to believe that he needed to go to college.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.020
They kind of just thought that my dream of being a mechanic was kind of like

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.500
one of those childhood dreams, they kind of thought that a mechanic wasn't a successful job,

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.780
they thought it was kind of like a lower-class job. So he enrolled at UC Irvine

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.780
with a plan to major in mechanical engineering.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:45.360
During his sophomore year Gamez dropped out because he realized he wasn't on the path he wanted.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.440
So I talked to my counselors, talked to my directors, I even talked to the president of UC Irvine,

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:54.640
I talked to every single person I could to simply want to stay at UC Irvine and do what I wanted to do.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.040
At the end of the day it was kind of like, you don't belong here.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.900
Public colleges and Universities should be tuition free and debt free for all Americans.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:09.360
Make college universally available with free tuition and fees.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.400
Several Democratic presidential candidates have promised that if elected, they'll make public college

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:21.360
tuition free and wipe clear federal student loan debt, which in the U.S. tops of 1.5 trillion dollars.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:27.020
They say that making college universal will lead to higher productivity and more economic opportunity,

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.400
for people like Michael Gamez. If you make College free, then there's gonna be so many BA's floating around

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.000
that if you want to get a better job then you're gonna need to go and get some supplemental degree.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.920
Bryan Caplan is an economist at George Mason University who's skeptical that professors like him

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:48.660
have much to offer most students. His book is called The Case Against Education.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:53.480
We're spending too much time and money on education because most of what you learn in school

00:01:53.480 --> 00:02:00.320
you will never use after the final exam. We like to pretend otherwise but really if you just calmly compare

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.180
what we're studying to what we really do the connection is shockingly weak.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:10.000
Caplan says that most people attend college as a way to signal to prospective employers

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.640
that they're reasonably intelligent, conscientious, and conformist.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.040
The signaling story is mostly that our society says that you're supposed to graduate,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:24.640
and if you're supposed to graduate, then the failure to graduate signals nonconformity.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.100
So if you want people to treat you well, you better go and conform.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.460
If you remember what it's like to be a student, you have to jump through an endless series

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.380
of seemingly meaningless, arbitrary, unfair hoops.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.560
And people that are willing to just bite their tongues and suffer through it, are the ones

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.520
who are also going to be good at doing that once they get a job.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:51.620
Caplan's case rests partly on the so-called sheepskin effect. Named for the sheepskin upon which diplomas

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:57.760
were once printed. Studies of college graduates earnings reveal that the salary increase for completing

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:05.040
the final year of college, is on average more than double that of completing all three of the previous years,

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.960
implying it's the fortitude to obtain the degree, not the knowledge gained that explains the boost

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.660
in compensation. The human capital view says that basically all of what's going on in schools

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:21.720
they are pouring useful skills into you they are preparing you for whatever job it is that you are going to get.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.520
What I'm saying is that the main payoff you're getting from school is that you're getting certified,

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.780
you're getting stamped, getting what you need to convince employers that you are a good bet.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:38.300
We are helping our individual participants build a very strong signal for themselves.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.460
As opposed to relying on you know, a third party large institution.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:49.700
Praxis combines a professional bootcamp with a paid apprenticeship. Cameron Sorsby is the CEO of Praxis,

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:54.600
a professional training program trying to offer an alternative to four-year university.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:04:02.780
The way I would describe our curriculum development process now is everything is market driven.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:10.440
We're constantly learning what hiring managers need to see in order to feel confident that they should hire

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.160
any individual participant. They're really good about shifting you from the mindset of

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.300
you're doing this for a grade, or you're doing this to get approval.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.280
They put all of the weight on you. Like what is your definition of success.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.400
Leah Wilczewski enrolled in Praxis one year program

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:34.360
focusing on communication, marketing and other jobs. It costs $12,000 but included a six-month

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:40.400
paid apprenticeship worth $16,000 meaning she'll finish the program $4,000 in the black.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:46.000
Wilczewski is working at Impossible Foods the Bay Area company that sells a meatless hamburger.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:56.940
I feel as if being in Praxis and being able to nail a job that typically requires four years of school if not more,

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:04.160
and knowing that there's a whole line of people who applied for the same job and being able to nail that

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:12.460
at only twenty-one and without that business college degree, it's like okay,

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.480
with that knowledge, what else can I do?

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.720
After Michael Gamez dropped out of UC Irvine, he enrolled in an auto mechanic trade school,

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.580
while also working a job at Pep Boys.

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:30.840
Then he applied for and received a $12,400 scholarship from Mike Rowe Works,

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:36.160
which looks for two qualities from applicants who were looking to enter the skilled trades:

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:41.320
the willingness to learn a useful skill, and the willingness to work your ass off.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:45.680
Combined we think that is something that ought to be affirmatively rewarded.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.240
From there, he entered a three-month training program with BMW and the day after finishing,

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:57.280
began his job as a high level technician at BMW of Beverly Hills.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.640
I get excited to go to work because it's something that I really like to do. So I feel like a lot of people,

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.040
they get surprised when I tell them the amount of money that a mechanic or a technician can make

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.740
at a dealership. They're like really? You guys make that much money? I'm like, yeah.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:11.460
So I feel like there's always this idea that if you don't go to college,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:15.300
you don't get a degree, you won't make a lot of money. I think that's wrong. It's kind of where you put yourself.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:20.680
Even though it's possible to acquire the necessary skills to make a good living without attending college,

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:25.460
enrollment at four-year universities has stayed steady  for the past ten years.

00:06:25.468 --> 00:06:29.020
Caplan remains skeptical that it will decline anytime soon.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.600
So I think things are gonna stay about the way they are. When you have that kind of free money

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.920
coming from people that are not actually paying out of their own pockets

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.680
that tends to freeze the world of in place.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:44.340
Maybe, maybe not, college has a place for, you know the average student going forward,

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.760
but I think what's most important is that people are just starting to question and starting to think more critically.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:56.000
What I'd like to see most is for young people to have more access to early professional opportunity

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:03.420
because I think gaining that early experience is the best way to learn what you do and and do not want to pursue.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.340
As for with Wilczewski and Gamez, she has two more months left in her apprenticeship

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:13.660
and hopes that Impossible Foods will keep her on in the sales department. And Gamez hopes

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:18.460
that working for BMW is a first step toward eventually owning his own shop.

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:21.600
One day you will see me in my own shop and they're like oh yeah ha, whatever. And I'm like,

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.880
no you really will. And the day that I invite you to my shop, then you'll see.

